🎯 Q10

Explain clearly how AWS evaluates permissions when multiple policies are attached.

🎯 Q10

Explain clearly how AWS evaluates permissions when multiple policies are attached.

✅ Strong Interview Answer (Say Like This)

When multiple IAM policies are attached to a user, group, or role, AWS evaluates them using a specific logic.

First, AWS checks if there is any explicit deny for the requested action. If an explicit deny exists in any policy, the request is immediately denied.

If there is no explicit deny, AWS then checks whether there is at least one explicit allow for that action. If an allow exists, the request is permitted.

If no policy explicitly allows the action, the request is denied by default. This is called implicit deny.

🧠 Simple Evaluation Flow (Remember This)

1️⃣ Check Explicit Deny → If yes → Deny
2️⃣ Check Allow → If yes → Allow
3️⃣ Otherwise → Implicit Deny

🎯 Important Points to Mention

AWS combines all attached policies.

Explicit deny always overrides allow.

Default behavior is deny.

🔥 Example You Can Give

For example, if one policy allows ec2:StartInstances and another policy denies ec2:StartInstances, AWS will deny the request because explicit deny has higher priority.

That shows practical understanding.

💡 Advanced (If Interviewer Goes Deeper)

In real-world evaluation, AWS may also consider:

Identity-based policies

Resource-based policies (like S3 bucket policy)

Permission boundaries

Service Control Policies (SCPs)

Session policies

And AWS evaluates them together before making the final decision.

